{"type":"message","id":"663ab3c9","parentId":"fe0416d9","timestamp":"2026-05-21T06:03:55.973Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_D0V75pSlfUqhcBPxEEv2mD22","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"south-carolina-bans-cbdc-protects-bitcoin-miners-2026-05-21\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"South Carolina bans CBDC participation and protects crypto users and Bitcoin miners\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"South Carolina crypto law\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"South Carolina Governor Signs Law Banning CBDC Payments and Protecting Bitcoin Miners\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Bitcoin\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"bitcoin\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinTelegraph\",\n    \"Decrypt\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/news/south-carolina-governor-signs-bill-protecting-bitcoin-miners-banning-cbdc\",\n    \"https://decrypt.co/368455/south-carolina-law-banning-cbdc-protecting-crypto-users-bitcoin-miners\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"Cointelegraph says Governor Henry McMaster signed Senate Bill 163 into law on Tuesday, making South Carolina one of the more crypto-friendly state frameworks in the US.\",\n    \"The law bars state agencies and political subdivisions from accepting or participating in CBDC payments or Federal Reserve-led digital currency trials.\",\n    \"The bill protects self-custody rights and prohibits governments from restricting hardware wallets or self-hosted wallets.\",\n    \"Bitcoin miners in industrial zones receive protections against discriminatory zoning and licensing rules, including noise limits beyond general pollution rules.\",\n    \"Decrypt reports the bill also exempts miners, node operators, blockchain software developers, and crypto-to-crypto traders from money transmitter licensing requirements.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"Cointelegraph RSS item:\\nSouth Carolina governor signs bill protecting Bitcoin miners, banning CBDC\\nPubDate: Wed, 20 May 2026 11:21:32 +0000\\nDescription: Senate Bill 163 bans CBDC payments by state agencies and shields Bitcoin miners from discriminatory zoning and licensing rules.\\n\\nCointelegraph article text:\\nSouth Carolina Governor Henry McMaster signed Senate Bill 163 into law on Tuesday, advancing one of the most crypto-friendly state-level frameworks in the country.\\nOn CBDCs, the law bars any state agency or political subdivision from accepting, requiring payment in, or participating in Federal Reserve-led digital currency trials, including any pilot programs run by federal agencies.\\nIt also protects crypto self-custody rights, preventing governments from restricting the use of hardware and self-hosted wallets while barring higher taxes on crypto transactions than comparable payments made in US dollars.\\nThe bill gives Bitcoin miners operating in industrial zones specific protections. Local governments cannot impose restrictions on mining businesses that do not apply to other industrial operations in the same area, and cannot set mining-specific noise limits beyond what general pollution rules already require.\\n\\u201cA political subdivision shall not change the zoning of a digital asset mining business without going through the proper notice and comment. A digital asset mining business may appeal a change in zoning to the proper court of jurisdiction,\\u201d the bill reads.\\nThe law also exempts several activities from money transmitter licensing requirements, including mining, node operation, blockchain software development and crypto-to-crypto trading. Mining-as-a-service and staking-as-a-service providers are excluded from securities classification.\\nOklahoma, Arkansas, Florida, Mississippi, Montana, North Dakota, Louisiana and Arizona are among the states that have passed similar pieces of crypto legislation in recent years.\\nCointelegraph meta summary:\\nSouth Carolina has passed S. Bill 163, banning state agencies from accepting CBDCs, protecting crypto self-custody rights, and exempting Bitcoin miners from money transmitter licensing requirements.\\n\\nDecrypt RSS item:\\nSouth Carolina Passes Law Banning CBDCs While Protecting Crypto Users, Bitcoin Miners - Decrypt\\nPubDate: Wed, 20 May 2026 15:08:53 GMT\\nDescription: The crypto-friendly legislation was signed into law on Tuesday, granting protections to crypto users, developers, and Bitcoin miners.\\n\\nDecrypt article text:\\nIn brief\\n- Senate Bill 163 was signed into law by South Carolina Governor Henry McMaster on Tuesday.\\n- The bill effectively bans the use of CBDCs in the state and protects authorities from participating in any CBDC tests.\\n- It also allows for the use and self-custody of crypto assets in the state, including offering protections to Bitcoin miners.\\nIndividuals, businesses, and Bitcoin miners in South Carolina have earned protections for the use, custody, and mining of digital assets thanks to a new bill signed by the state\\u2019s governor Henry McMaster.\\nFirst introduced in January 2025, Bill 163, also effectively bans the state\\u2019s use of any central bank digital currency (CBDC), also known as a \\\"digital dollar,\\\" and its participation in any CBDC tests.\\n\\u201cAn individual or business shall not be prohibited, restricted, or otherwise prevented from accepting digital assets to purchase legal goods or services; or using a self-hosted wallet or hardware wallet, to maintain self-custody of digital assets,\\u201d the bill reads.\\nIt also notes that restrictions cannot be placed on digital asset mining businesses in areas that are designed for industrial use, so long as they comply with general sound pollution limits and don\\u2019t place \\u201cadditional stress on the electrical grid.\\u201d\\nThe bill\\u2019s final section indicates that a money transmitter license is not required for miners, node operators, or those developing blockchain software.\\nThe bill was passed by the Senate in April 2025 and then referred to the House Labor, Commerce and Industry Committee that May\\u2014which came back with a favorable report nearly a year later, this April. In early May, a House vote registered 110 \\u201cyeas\\u201d and 1 \\u201cnay.\\u201d\\nThe piece of crypto-friendly legislation is not the only one to hit the general assembly in the state. Last year, a South Carolina representative introduced the Strategic Digital Assets Reserve Act, also identified as House Bill 4256.\\nThat bill proposed that the state\\u2019s treasurer be allowed to allocate up to 10% of select public funds into Bitcoin, as the general assembly found \\u201cinflation has eroded the purchasing power of assets held in state funds managed by the State Treasurer, as well as state retirement funds.\\u201d\\nThe bill was referred to the Committee on Ways and Means in March 2025, but has not been further updated.\\nA representative for South Carolina Governor Henry McMaster did not immediately respond to Decrypt\\u2019s request for comment.\\n\\nCombined context from both sources:\\nSouth Carolina\\u2019s new law is being framed as a broader pro-crypto policy package rather than a narrow anti-CBDC measure. Both outlets emphasize that the statute protects self-custody, shields mining operations from local discriminatory treatment, and removes licensing friction for several categories of blockchain actors. The bill\\u2019s practical impact is that state and local agencies cannot join or require CBDC experiments, while Bitcoin miners and adjacent infrastructure providers gain legal clarity around zoning, industrial-use protections, and money transmitter exemptions. The Decrypt report adds legislative background, noting the bill\\u2019s long path through the Senate, House committee review, and final House vote. It also highlights a separate reserve proposal that would have let South Carolina allocate up to 10% of certain public funds into Bitcoin, signaling a broader policy interest in digital assets.\\n\\nRelated policy details worth preserving:\\nThe bill was first introduced in January 2025.\\nThe House vote in early May registered 110 yeas and 1 nay.\\nThe law covers self-hosted wallets, hardware wallets, mining businesses, node operators, and blockchain software developers.\\nThe state cannot participate in CBDC tests or Federal Reserve-led digital currency trials.\\n\",\n  \"tweet_quotes\": []\n}"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":8,"aggregated":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"south-carolina-bans-cbdc-protects-bitcoin-miners-2026-05-21\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"South Carolina bans CBDC participation and protects crypto users and Bitcoin miners\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"South Carolina crypto law\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"South Carolina Governor Signs Law Banning CBDC Payments and Protecting Bitcoin Miners\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Bitcoin\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"bitcoin\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinTelegraph\",\n    \"Decrypt\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/news/south-carolina-governor-signs-bill-protecting-bitcoin-miners-banning-cbdc\",\n    \"https://decrypt.co/368455/south-carolina-law-banning-cbdc-protecting-crypto-users-bitcoin-miners\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"Cointelegraph says Governor Henry McMaster signed Senate Bill 163 into law on Tuesday, making South Carolina one of the more crypto-friendly state frameworks in the US.\",\n    \"The law bars state agencies and political subdivisions from accepting or participating in CBDC payments or Federal Reserve-led digital currency trials.\",\n    \"The bill protects self-custody rights and prohibits governments from restricting hardware wallets or self-hosted wallets.\",\n    \"Bitcoin miners in industrial zones receive protections against discriminatory zoning and licensing rules, including noise limits beyond general pollution rules.\",\n    \"Decrypt reports the bill also exempts miners, node operators, blockchain software developers, and crypto-to-crypto traders from money transmitter licensing requirements.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"Cointelegraph RSS item:\\nSouth Carolina governor signs bill protecting Bitcoin miners, banning CBDC\\nPubDate: Wed, 20 May 2026 11:21:32 +0000\\nDescription: Senate Bill 163 bans CBDC payments by state agencies and shields Bitcoin miners from discriminatory zoning and licensing rules.\\n\\nCointelegraph article text:\\nSouth Carolina Governor Henry McMaster signed Senate Bill 163 into law on Tuesday, advancing one of the most crypto-friendly state-level frameworks in the country.\\nOn CBDCs, the law bars any state agency or political subdivision from accepting, requiring payment in, or participating in Federal Reserve-led digital currency trials, including any pilot programs run by federal agencies.\\nIt also protects crypto self-custody rights, preventing governments from restricting the use of hardware and self-hosted wallets while barring higher taxes on crypto transactions than comparable payments made in US dollars.\\nThe bill gives Bitcoin miners operating in industrial zones specific protections. Local governments cannot impose restrictions on mining businesses that do not apply to other industrial operations in the same area, and cannot set mining-specific noise limits beyond what general pollution rules already require.\\n\\u201cA political subdivision shall not change the zoning of a digital asset mining business without going through the proper notice and comment. A digital asset mining business may appeal a change in zoning to the proper court of jurisdiction,\\u201d the bill reads.\\nThe law also exempts several activities from money transmitter licensing requirements, including mining, node operation, blockchain software development and crypto-to-crypto trading. Mining-as-a-service and staking-as-a-service providers are excluded from securities classification.\\nOklahoma, Arkansas, Florida, Mississippi, Montana, North Dakota, Louisiana and Arizona are among the states that have passed similar pieces of crypto legislation in recent years.\\nCointelegraph meta summary:\\nSouth Carolina has passed S. Bill 163, banning state agencies from accepting CBDCs, protecting crypto self-custody rights, and exempting Bitcoin miners from money transmitter licensing requirements.\\n\\nDecrypt RSS item:\\nSouth Carolina Passes Law Banning CBDCs While Protecting Crypto Users, Bitcoin Miners - Decrypt\\nPubDate: Wed, 20 May 2026 15:08:53 GMT\\nDescription: The crypto-friendly legislation was signed into law on Tuesday, granting protections to crypto users, developers, and Bitcoin miners.\\n\\nDecrypt article text:\\nIn brief\\n- Senate Bill 163 was signed into law by South Carolina Governor Henry McMaster on Tuesday.\\n- The bill effectively bans the use of CBDCs in the state and protects authorities from participating in any CBDC tests.\\n- It also allows for the use and self-custody of crypto assets in the state, including offering protections to Bitcoin miners.\\nIndividuals, businesses, and Bitcoin miners in South Carolina have earned protections for the use, custody, and mining of digital assets thanks to a new bill signed by the state\\u2019s governor Henry McMaster.\\nFirst introduced in January 2025, Bill 163, also effectively bans the state\\u2019s use of any central bank digital currency (CBDC), also known as a \\\"digital dollar,\\\" and its participation in any CBDC tests.\\n\\u201cAn individual or business shall not be prohibited, restricted, or otherwise prevented from accepting digital assets to purchase legal goods or services; or using a self-hosted wallet or hardware wallet, to maintain self-custody of digital assets,\\u201d the bill reads.\\nIt also notes that restrictions cannot be placed on digital asset mining businesses in areas that are designed for industrial use, so long as they comply with general sound pollution limits and don\\u2019t place \\u201cadditional stress on the electrical grid.\\u201d\\nThe bill\\u2019s final section indicates that a money transmitter license is not required for miners, node operators, or those developing blockchain software.\\nThe bill was passed by the Senate in April 2025 and then referred to the House Labor, Commerce and Industry Committee that May\\u2014which came back with a favorable report nearly a year later, this April. In early May, a House vote registered 110 \\u201cyeas\\u201d and 1 \\u201cnay.\\u201d\\nThe piece of crypto-friendly legislation is not the only one to hit the general assembly in the state. Last year, a South Carolina representative introduced the Strategic Digital Assets Reserve Act, also identified as House Bill 4256.\\nThat bill proposed that the state\\u2019s treasurer be allowed to allocate up to 10% of select public funds into Bitcoin, as the general assembly found \\u201cinflation has eroded the purchasing power of assets held in state funds managed by the State Treasurer, as well as state retirement funds.\\u201d\\nThe bill was referred to the Committee on Ways and Means in March 2025, but has not been further updated.\\nA representative for South Carolina Governor Henry McMaster did not immediately respond to Decrypt\\u2019s request for comment.\\n\\nCombined context from both sources:\\nSouth Carolina\\u2019s new law is being framed as a broader pro-crypto policy package rather than a narrow anti-CBDC measure. Both outlets emphasize that the statute protects self-custody, shields mining operations from local discriminatory treatment, and removes licensing friction for several categories of blockchain actors. The bill\\u2019s practical impact is that state and local agencies cannot join or require CBDC experiments, while Bitcoin miners and adjacent infrastructure providers gain legal clarity around zoning, industrial-use protections, and money transmitter exemptions. The Decrypt report adds legislative background, noting the bill\\u2019s long path through the Senate, House committee review, and final House vote. It also highlights a separate reserve proposal that would have let South Carolina allocate up to 10% of certain public funds into Bitcoin, signaling a broader policy interest in digital assets.\\n\\nRelated policy details worth preserving:\\nThe bill was first introduced in January 2025.\\nThe House vote in early May registered 110 yeas and 1 nay.\\nThe law covers self-hosted wallets, hardware wallets, mining businesses, node operators, and blockchain software developers.\\nThe state cannot participate in CBDC tests or Federal Reserve-led digital currency trials.\\n\",\n  \"tweet_quotes\": []\n}","cwd":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-writer"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1779343435973}}
